</corresp><fn id="bju70015-note-0001"><label>&#x02020;</label><p>J.B. and J.C. are co&#x02010;first authors.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>30</day><month>9</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>1</month><year>2026</year></pub-date><volume>137</volume><issue seq="110">1</issue><issue-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/bju.v137.1</issue-id><fpage>49</fpage><lpage>57</lpage><permissions><!--Copyright &#x000a9; 2026 BJU International--><copyright-statement content-type="article-copyright">&#x000a9; 2025 The Author(s). <italic toggle="yes">BJU International</italic> published by John Wiley &#x00026; Sons Ltd on behalf of BJU International.</copyright-statement><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link> License, which permits use and distribution in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited, the use is non&#x02010;commercial and no modifications or adaptations are made.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri content-type="pdf" xlink:href="file:BJU-137-49.pdf"/><abstract><sec id="bju70015-sec-0001"><title>Objective</title><p>To synthesise data evaluating the diagnostic utility of prostate&#x02010;specific membrane antigen (PSMA) positron emission tomography/computed tomography (PET/CT) in intermediate&#x02010;risk prostate cancer (PCa), as its role in intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa remains uncertain.</p></sec><sec id="bju70015-sec-0002"><title>Methods</title><p>A systematic search was conducted for studies evaluating PSMA PET/CT in primary staging of newly diagnosed European Association of Urology (EAU)/National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) intermediate&#x02010;risk, or Gleason Score 7 (International Society of Urological Pathology [ISUP] Grade Group [GG] 2&#x02013;3) PCa. The primary outcome was overall positive scans (lymph nodes or distant metastases). We also secondarily analysed diagnostic accuracy measuring sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, and negative predictive value.</p></sec><sec id="bju70015-sec-0003"><title>Results</title><p>In total, 16 studies reported positivity rates ranging from 2.2% to 50.0% in intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa. A pooled analysis of 13 studies found a PSMA PET/CT positivity rate of 9% (95% confidence interval [CI] 6&#x02013;11%). In ISUP GG 2 PCa, positivity ranged from 2.2% to 21.4%, compared to 13.6&#x02013;33.3% in ISUP GG 3 PCa (across two studies). The overall nodal and metastatic sensitivity of PSMA PET/CT was low in intermediate&#x02010;risk men, 33% (95% CI 14&#x02013;60%). Limitations included small sample sizes, retrospective designs, and limited histopathological confirmation.</p></sec><sec id="bju70015-sec-0004"><title>Conclusion</title><p>For primary staging in intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa PSMA PET/CT showed a low yield. Limited data exist to guide its use in favourable vs unfavourable subgroups. Robust, prospective studies are needed to define its role in clinical decision&#x02010;making, further prognostic benefits, and to inform future guidelines.</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group kwd-group-type="author-generated"><kwd id="bju70015-kwd-0005">Conventional Imaging</kwd><kwd id="bju70015-kwd-0001">Gleason Score 7</kwd><kwd id="bju70015-kwd-0002">intermediate&#x02010;risk prostate cancer</kwd><kwd id="bju70015-kwd-0003">prostate cancer</kwd><kwd id="bju70015-kwd-0004">PSMA PET/CT</kwd></kwd-group><counts><fig-count count="2"/><table-count count="1"/><page-count count="9"/><word-count count="6882"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>January 2026</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.6.6 mode:remove_FC converted:10.12.2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body id="bju70015-body-0001"><def-list list-content="abbreviations" id="bju70015-dl-0001"><title>Abbreviations</title><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0001">ANZCTR</term><def id="bju70015-li-0002"><p>Australian New Zealand Clinical Trials Registry</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0003">CVI</term><def id="bju70015-li-0004"><p>conventional imaging</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0005">EAU</term><def id="bju70015-li-0006"><p>European Association of Urology</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0007">GG</term><def id="bju70015-li-0008"><p>Grade Group</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0009">IQR</term><def id="bju70015-li-0010"><p>interquartile range</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0011">ISUP</term><def id="bju70015-li-0012"><p>International Society of Urological Pathology</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0013">NPV</term><def id="bju70015-li-0014"><p>negative predictive value</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0015">PCa</term><def id="bju70015-li-0016"><p>prostate cancer</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0017">PET</term><def id="bju70015-li-0018"><p>position emission tomography</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0019">PLND</term><def id="bju70015-li-0020"><p>pelvic lymph node dissection</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0021">PPV</term><def id="bju70015-li-0022"><p>positive predictive value</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0023">PRISMA</term><def id="bju70015-li-0024"><p>Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta&#x02010;analyses</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0025">PSMA</term><def id="bju70015-li-0026"><p>prostate&#x02010;specific membrane antigen</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0027">RCT</term><def id="bju70015-li-0028"><p>randomised controlled trial</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="bju70015-li-0029">SUV<sub>max</sub>
</term><def id="bju70015-li-0030"><p>maximum standardised uptake value</p></def></def-item></def-list><sec id="bju70015-sec-0005"><title>Introduction</title><p>Recently, Australian evidence demonstrating the superior accuracy of prostate&#x02010;specific membrane antigen (PSMA) positron emission tomography/computed tomography (PET/CT) over conventional imaging (CVI) for diagnosis and staging of high&#x02010;risk prostate cancer (PCa) [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>] was central to updated recommendations in the European Association of Urology (EAU) guidelines [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>]. As a result of these updates, the Australian Federal Government announced that the Medicare Benefits Schedule (MBS) will fund a whole&#x02010;body PSMA PET/CT for primary staging in untreated patients with intermediate&#x02010; or high&#x02010;risk PCa, aiming for curative intent.</p><p>Despite a high utility for PSMA PET/CT in high&#x02010;risk PCa, limited data exists in its use in staging men with intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>]. The current literature supports the guideline&#x02010;recommended use of PSMA PET/CT for unfavourable intermediate&#x02010;risk or higher in the initial staging [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>]. The proPSMA trial (Australian New Zealand Clinical Trials Registry [ANZCTR] identifier: ACTRN12617000005358) demonstrated greater accuracy in detecting metastases compared with CVI in men with high&#x02010;risk PCa [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>], although only a handful of studies have investigated the utility of PSMA PET/CT in staging for men with intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>]. This gap in the literature has also led to a discordance in imaging recommendations between guidelines where the EAU and the National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) favours the use of PSMA PET/CT in unfavourable intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>]; however, a recent review of the guidelines demonstrated lack of consensus regarding whether PSMA PET/CT should be used to stage intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>].</p><p>This review aimed to evaluate the utility of PSMA PET/CT in the primary staging of newly diagnosed intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa. We measured the incidence of positive scans in patients with intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa and also assessed the diagnostic accuracy of PSMA PET/CT in this group.</p></sec><sec sec-type="methods" id="bju70015-sec-0006"><title>Methods</title><sec id="bju70015-sec-0007"><title>Search Strategy</title><p>This systematic review and meta&#x02010;analysis were prospectively registered in the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews database (PROSPERO number: CRD420251022849) and adhered to the guidelines described in the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta&#x02010;analyses (PRISMA) 2020 statement [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>].</p><p>A comprehensive and systematic search of electronic databases, including the Cumulative Index to Nursing and Allied Health Literature (CINAHL), Excerpta Medica dataBASE (EMBASE) and Medical Literature Analysis and Retrieval System Online (MEDLINE), was conducted in March 2025 without imposing any date restrictions. Search terms included &#x02018;prostate&#x02010;specific membrane antigen&#x02019;, &#x02018;PSMA&#x02019;, &#x02018;prostate cancer&#x02019;, &#x02018;prostate neoplasm&#x02019;, &#x02018;intermediate risk&#x02019;, &#x02018;positron emission tomography&#x02019;, and &#x02018;PET&#x02019; (Figs&#x000a0;<xref rid="bju70015-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S1</xref> and <xref rid="bju70015-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S2</xref>). In addition, this search was complemented by a cited reference screen, by looking through reference lists of included papers and utilising Connected Papers.</p></sec><sec id="bju70015-sec-0008"><title>Study Selection</title><p>Automated duplicate article detection was utilised on Covidence (Veritas Health Innovation Ltd, Melbourne, Victoria, Australia), with additional duplicates noted on screening. Studies were only included if data were stratified to accurately determine positive PSMA PET/CT on primary staging in intermediate&#x02010;risk, Gleason Score 7, or International Society of Urological Pathology (ISUP) Grade Group (GG) 2 or 3 PCa. All randomised controlled trials (RCTs), retrospective and prospective cohort studies were included. Studies were excluded if they evaluated PSMA PET/CT in re&#x02010;staging for biochemical recurrence, or did not perform PSMA PET/CT for primary staging. Conference abstracts and posters, editorial comments, case reports, literature reviews, and letters to the editor were excluded.</p><p>Data were extracted from the 16 articles with the assistance of Covidence, including sample size, average or median age of participants, study design, indication for imaging, the type of radiotracer used, image analysis, and positive metastases detected by PSMA PET/CT by classification (i.e., Gleason Score 7, ISUP GG 2&#x02013;3, intermediate&#x02010;risk). Additional data from Hofman et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>] was also obtained by contacting the authors.</p><p>Each part of the review process was independently performed by two authors, and any disagreements were resolved with senior author review.</p></sec><sec id="bju70015-sec-0009"><title>Data Extraction</title><p>Baseline study characteristics, PSMA radiotracer used, and the number of positive nodes or metastases on PSMA PET/CT, including true positives, false positives, true negatives and false negatives, when compared to an appropriate reference standard where reported.</p></sec><sec id="bju70015-sec-0010"><title>Endpoint Definition</title><p>The primary outcome included is the rate of detection of nodal (molecular imaging [mi]N1) or metastatic (M1) disease on PSMA PET/CT in each corresponding group: ISUP GG 2&#x02013;3, intermediate&#x02010;risk or Gleason Score 7. In addition, sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value (PPV), and negative predictive value (NPV) of PSMA PET/CT for primary staging of intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa was also analysed where there was sufficient data. Sensitivity, specificity, PPV, and NPV were extracted if reported, but otherwise calculated using the number of true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives where reported.</p></sec><sec id="bju70015-sec-0011"><title>Risk of Bias Assessment</title><p>The risk of bias was assessed using the Quality Assessment of Diagnostic Accuracy Studies 2 (QUADAS&#x02010;2) tool (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="bju70015-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S3</xref>).</p></sec><sec id="bju70015-sec-0012"><title>Statistical Analysis</title><p>Statistical analysis was performed using a random&#x02010;effects meta&#x02010;analysis model to account for heterogeneity across included studies. The primary endpoint was the pooled positivity rate of PSMA PET/CT for detecting nodal or distant metastases in intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa. Proportions were transformed using the Freeman&#x02013;Tukey double arcsine method to stabilise variances. Between&#x02010;study heterogeneity was quantified using the <italic toggle="yes">I</italic>
<sup>2</sup> statistic, <italic toggle="yes">&#x003c4;</italic>
<sup>2</sup> (tau&#x02010;squared), and Cochran's <italic toggle="yes">Q</italic> test. Heterogeneity was considered &#x02018;low&#x02019;, &#x02018;moderate&#x02019;, or &#x02018;high&#x02019; based on <italic toggle="yes">I</italic>
<sup>2</sup> thresholds of &#x0003c;25%, 25&#x02013;50%, and &#x0003e;50%, respectively.</p><p>Subgroup analysis was performed where data were available for favourable and unfavourable intermediate&#x02010;risk disease and included as separate studies. This was also performed based on ISUP GG and whether histopathological confirmation was used for the reference standard. There were insufficient studies to perform a meta&#x02010;analysis on these subgroup analyses and therefore data were not pooled and a qualitative approach was taken for presentation of data. Forest plots were generated for individual study positivity rates, as well as pooled sensitivity and specificity where sufficient data allowed. Sensitivity and specificity were extracted directly or calculated from reported 2&#x02009;&#x000d7;&#x02009;2 tables where available.</p><p>All analyses were conducted using R software (R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria) and the &#x02018;mada&#x02019; and &#x02018;metafor&#x02019; packages. A <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.05 was considered statistically significant.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results" id="bju70015-sec-0013"><title>Results</title><sec id="bju70015-sec-0014"><title>Study Selection</title><p>The systematic search yielded 527 articles, and these were screened by title and abstract to identify eligible articles. There were 16 studies selected for evidence synthesis, as per the PRISMA flowchart (Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="bju70015-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S4</xref>) [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">24</xref>, <xref rid="bju70015-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>]. The analysed papers were all retrospective cohort studies, except for Hofman et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>], which was a RCT. Included studies were separated based on how data were reported: intermediate&#x02010;risk, Gleason Score 7 or ISUP GG 2&#x02013;3. Data from patients with Gleason Score 7 (3&#x02009;+&#x02009;4) PCa in Hofman et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>] were provided by the authors directly with specific T (tumour) stage and PSA values for each patient.</p></sec><sec id="bju70015-sec-0015"><title>Study Characteristics</title><p>In total, there were 2223 patients with PCa with combined ISUP GG 2&#x02013;3, intermediate&#x02010;risk and Gleason Score 7 across the included 16 studies. Among these patients, the majority of them were in the intermediate&#x02010;risk group (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="bju70015-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>). Participant's median age ranged from 63 to 73&#x02009;years across studies. All studies were retrospective in design and used PSMA PET/CT to assess primary staging of PCa after biopsy, with the exception of Moradi et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="bju70015-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>], which used PSMA PET/MRI. In all, 12 studies also used the [<sup>68</sup>Ga]Ga&#x02010;PSMA&#x02010;11 radiotracer, with one study using the [<sup>18</sup>F]F&#x02010;PSMA1007, and three studies using a combination. Further, most studies used a minimum of two nuclear physicians to analyse images; however, some studies either did not report how images were analysed or did not report the number of physicians involved in this process.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="bju70015-tbl-0001" content-type="Table"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Articles that report positive metastases detected on PSMA PET/CT for primary staging in men with intermediate&#x02010;risk PCa.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="left" span="1"/><thead valign="top"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">References</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">N</italic>
